<section xml:id="sec-mod1-study-guide">
    <title>Companion Reading and Study Guide</title>
    <p>Reading: Hirst, Chapters 2<ndash/>3; Zorn, Sections 1.4<ndash/>1.5. Estimated time: 55 minutes.</p>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->

        <p>
          <alert>How to use this guide.</alert> Work through it
          <em>while</em> reading, not after. Each section names the
          assignment, explains what to look for, and poses
          <q>stop and check</q> questions.
        </p>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->

      <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod1-sg-z14">
        <title>Reading 1: Zorn &#xa7;1.4 <q>Proofs 101</q> (Suggested time: 20 minutes)</title>
        <p>
          Zorn treats proof-writing as a language skill. He opens with
          concrete prose rules (use standard notation; define everything;
          write complete <q>sentences</q>; watch word order; make it look
          easy; etc.) and then builds up a lexicon of logical terms.
          As you read, identify each rule and be able to say why it matters.
        </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-sg-z14-1">
        <title>Correcting a Malformed Statement</title>
        <statement>
          <p> Zorn writes that <q>For all <m>\Q</m>,
            we have <m>x^2 \geq 0</m></q> is nonsensical. Explain why
            this sentence fails, and write a corrected version.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            The statement fails because <m>\Q</m> denotes a set, not a
            variable. The quantifier <q>for all</q> must bind a variable
            ranging over a domain, not the domain itself. A corrected version:
            <q>For all <m>x \in \Q</m>, we have <m>x^2 \geq 0</m>.</q>
            In symbols: <m>\forall x \in \Q,\; x^2 \geq 0</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-sg-z14-2">
        <title>Word Order and Quantifiers</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Zorn's <q>Watch word order</q> section gives two statements
            involving <m>\varepsilon</m> and <m>n</m> that look similar but
            have opposite truth values. Write out both statements, and
            explain why their quantifier order makes them different.
            (This pair recurs in <xref ref="ch-mod4" text="custom">Module 4</xref> on sequences.)
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            The two statements (over <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m> and <m>n \in \N</m>) are:
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                <m>\forall \varepsilon \gt 0 \;\; \exists n \in \N
                \text{ such that } \tfrac{1}{n} \lt \varepsilon</m>.
                <em>True</em> (Archimedean property).
              </li>
              <li>
                <m>\exists n \in \N \text{ such that }
                \forall \varepsilon \gt 0,\;\; \tfrac{1}{n} \lt \varepsilon</m>.
                <em>False</em>: no fixed <m>n</m> makes <m>1/n</m> smaller
                than every positive <m>\varepsilon</m>.
              </li>
            </ol>
            The difference is which variable is fixed first. In (a) <m>n</m> may
            depend on <m>\varepsilon</m>; in (b) <m>n</m> must work for all
            <m>\varepsilon</m> simultaneously.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-sg-z14-3">
        <title>Scratch Work vs. Finished Proof</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The <q>Make it look easy</q> rule says a finished proof should
            hide the scratch work. Does this mean you should not show your
            reasoning? Explain the distinction Zorn is drawing.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            Zorn is not saying to omit reasoning; he is saying that the
            <em>finished proof</em> should read as a clean, forward-moving
            argument rather than a record of how you found the proof. Scratch
            work (trying cases, working backwards, computing examples) is
            essential privately, but the write-up presents the logical chain
            in order, without dead ends or self-corrections. In MAT 5610,
            assessments will ask you to include clearly labeled scratch work
            before the formal proof.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-sg-z14-4">
        <title>De Morgan's Laws for Quantifiers</title>
        <statement>
          <p> State De Morgan's laws for <em>quantified</em>
            statements (not just for <q>and</q>/<q>or</q>). Use the
            symbols <m>\forall</m>, <m>\exists</m>, and <m>\neg</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            The quantifier De Morgan laws (where <m>P(x)</m> is a predicate
            over domain <m>D</m>):
            <md>
              <mrow>\neg\bigl(\forall x \in D,\; P(x)\bigr) \amp\iff \exists x \in D,\; \neg P(x)</mrow>
              <mrow>\neg\bigl(\exists x \in D,\; P(x)\bigr) \amp\iff \forall x \in D,\; \neg P(x)</mrow>
            </md>
            In words: the negation of a universal is an existential counterexample;
            the negation of an existential is a universal denial.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

        <p>
          Negating a quantified statement flips each quantifier. Two further
          rules come up whenever you negate a definition from analysis.
          First, the negation of an implication is not another implication:
          <md>
            \neg(P \implies Q) \iff P \wedge \neg Q,
          </md>
          since an implication is false exactly when its hypothesis is true
          and its conclusion is false. Second, a quantifier with a condition
          attached, such as <m>\forall \varepsilon \gt 0</m>, keeps its
          condition under negation:
          <md>
            \neg\bigl(\forall \varepsilon \gt 0,\; P(\varepsilon)\bigr) \iff \exists \varepsilon \gt 0,\; \neg P(\varepsilon).
          </md>
          The reason is that <m>\forall \varepsilon \gt 0,\; P(\varepsilon)</m>
          is shorthand for <m>\forall \varepsilon \bigl(\varepsilon \gt 0 \implies P(\varepsilon)\bigr)</m>.
          By the first rule, its negation is
          <m>\exists \varepsilon \bigl(\varepsilon \gt 0 \wedge \neg P(\varepsilon)\bigr)</m>.
          The negation still speaks about positive <m>\varepsilon</m>; it
          does not produce <m>\varepsilon \leq 0</m>.
        </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-sg-z14-5">
        <title>Archimedean Property in Symbols</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Zorn gives an example of the Archimedean property using
            quantifiers. Write it out in full symbolic form and explain
            in one sentence what it means. (You will prove this in <xref ref="ch-mod2"/>.)
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <m>\forall x \in \R,\; \exists n \in \N \text{ such that } x \lt n</m>.
            This says: every real number is exceeded by some natural number,
            i.e., the natural numbers are unbounded above in <m>\R</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod1-sg-archimedean">
        <title>Three Forms of the Archimedean Property</title>
        <p>
          This module uses the Archimedean property in three forms:
          <ol marker="(i)">
            <li>
              <m>\forall x \in \R \;\; \exists n \in \N</m> such that
              <m>n \gt x</m> (the form in <xref ref="ex-mod1-sg-z14-5"/>);
            </li>
            <li>
              <m>\forall \varepsilon \gt 0 \;\; \exists n \in \N</m>
              such that <m>1/n \lt \varepsilon</m> (the form in
              <xref ref="ex-mod1-we-1"/>);
            </li>
            <li>
              for all positive real numbers <m>\varepsilon</m> and
              <m>x</m> there is an <m>n \in \N</m> such that
              <m>n\varepsilon \gt x</m> (Bauldry's Theorem 2.3, which you
              will write in symbols in <xref ref="ex-mod1-br-5"/>).
            </li>
          </ol>
          They are different statements, but the implications
          (i) <m>\Rightarrow</m> (iii) <m>\Rightarrow</m> (ii)
          <m>\Rightarrow</m> (i) run around a cycle, so all three are
          equivalent.
          <ul>
            <li>
              (i) implies (iii): given <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m> and
              <m>x \gt 0</m>, apply (i) to the real number
              <m>x/\varepsilon</m> to get <m>n \gt x/\varepsilon</m>, and
              multiply by <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>.
            </li>
            <li>
              (iii) implies (ii): given <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>, apply
              (iii) with <m>x = 1</m> to get <m>n\varepsilon \gt 1</m>, and
              divide by <m>n \gt 0</m>.
            </li>
            <li>
              (ii) implies (i): given <m>x \in \R</m>, if <m>x \leq 0</m>
              then <m>n = 1</m> works; if <m>x \gt 0</m>, apply (ii) with
              <m>\varepsilon = 1/x</m> to get <m>1/n \lt 1/x</m>, which
              gives <m>n \gt x</m> because both sides are positive.
            </li>
          </ul>
          These arguments show only that the three forms stand or fall
          together; none of them proves the property. In this module the
          property is taken as known. <xref ref="ch-mod2"/> derives it from
          the Completeness Axiom of <m>\R</m>.
        </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-sg-z14-6">
        <title>Converse and Contrapositive</title>
        <statement>
          <p> For the implication <q>If <m>f</m> is
            differentiable at <m>a</m>, then <m>f</m> is continuous
            at <m>a</m></q>:
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>State the converse. Is it true?</li>
              <li>State the contrapositive. Is it true?</li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                <alert>Converse:</alert> <q>If <m>f</m> is continuous at
                <m>a</m>, then <m>f</m> is differentiable at <m>a</m>.</q>
                <em>False.</em> The function <m>f(x) = |x|</m> is continuous
                at <m>0</m> but not differentiable there.
              </li>
              <li>
                <alert>Contrapositive:</alert> <q>If <m>f</m> is not
                continuous at <m>a</m>, then <m>f</m> is not differentiable
                at <m>a</m>.</q> <em>True</em> (logically equivalent to the
                original implication, which is a standard theorem).
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->

      <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod1-sg-z15">
        <title>Reading 2: Zorn &#xa7;1.5 <q>Types of Proof</q> (Suggested time: 20 minutes)</title>
        <p>
          Read this after Videos 2 and 3. Zorn gives well-chosen examples
          of all major proof strategies. The examples reinforce what you
          saw on the lightboard.
        </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-sg-z15-1">
        <title>Irrationality of <m>\sqrt{5}</m></title>
        <statement>
          <p> Zorn proves that <m>\sqrt{5}</m> is irrational
            using an argument about prime factorizations (rather than the
            parity argument). In your own words, describe the key idea.
            What property of squaring makes the argument work?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            The proof assumes <m>\sqrt{5} = p/q</m> in lowest terms, so
            <m>p^2 = 5q^2</m>. Since 5 is prime, <m>5 \mid p^2</m> implies
            <m>5 \mid p</m>, so write <m>p = 5k</m>; then <m>25k^2 = 5q^2</m>,
            giving <m>5 \mid q</m>. This contradicts <m>\gcd(p,q)=1</m>.
            Squaring preserves prime divisibility: if <m>r \mid a^2</m> and
            <m>r</m> is prime, then <m>r \mid a</m> (by unique factorization).
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-sg-z15-2">
        <title>Infinitely Many Primes</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Zorn proves there are infinitely many primes by contradiction.
            Trace through the proof: what is the assumption, what
            contradicting object is constructed, and what property of
            that object creates the contradiction?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <alert>Assumption:</alert> There are finitely many primes,
            <m>p_1, p_2, \ldots, p_k</m>. <alert>Constructed object:</alert>
            <m>N = p_1 p_2 \cdots p_k + 1</m>. <alert>Contradiction:</alert>
            Every prime <m>p_i</m> divides <m>p_1 \cdots p_k</m>, so
            <m>p_i \nmid N</m> (it leaves remainder 1). But <m>N \gt 1</m>
            must have some prime factor&#x2014;a prime not on our list. This
            contradicts the assumption that the list was complete.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-sg-z15-3">
        <title>Induction: Subsets</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Zorn's Claim VIII: a set with <m>n</m> elements has <m>2^n</m>
            subsets. Identify the base case, the induction hypothesis, and
            the key idea in the inductive step. (Hint: adding one element
            to a set does something predictable to the number of subsets.)
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <alert>Base case:</alert> <m>n = 0</m>. A set with 0 elements
            has exactly 1 subset (the empty set), and <m>2^0 = 1</m>. &#x2713;
          </p>
          <p>
            <alert>Induction hypothesis:</alert> Assume any set with <m>n</m>
            elements has <m>2^n</m> subsets.
          </p>
          <p>
            <alert>Inductive step:</alert> Let <m>A</m> have <m>n+1</m>
            elements; pick any element <m>a \in A</m>. Every subset of
            <m>A</m> either contains <m>a</m> or does not. The subsets not
            containing <m>a</m> are exactly the subsets of <m>A \setminus \{a\}</m>&#x2014;by
            the induction hypothesis, there are <m>2^n</m> of these.
            The subsets containing <m>a</m> are obtained by adding <m>a</m>
            to each subset of <m>A \setminus \{a\}</m>&#x2014;also <m>2^n</m>.
            Total: <m>2 \cdot 2^n = 2^{n+1}</m>. <m>\square</m>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod1-sg-strong-induction">
        <title>Strong Induction</title>
        <p>
          In ordinary induction, the inductive step assumes <m>P(n)</m> and
          proves <m>P(n+1)</m>. Some arguments for <m>P(n+1)</m> need the
          statement for a smaller value than <m>n</m>, or for several
          smaller values at once. <term>Strong induction</term> allows the
          inductive step to assume <m>P(k)</m> for every <m>k</m> from the
          base case up to <m>n</m>. Despite its name, it proves nothing
          that ordinary induction cannot: the proof below derives it from
          ordinary induction.
        </p>
        <theorem xml:id="thm-mod1-strong-induction">
          <title>Principle of Strong Induction</title>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Let <m>n_0</m> be an integer, and let <m>P(n)</m> be a
              statement about integers <m>n \geq n_0</m>. Suppose that
              <ol marker="(a)">
                <li><m>P(n_0)</m> holds, and</li>
                <li>
                  for every integer <m>n \geq n_0</m>, if <m>P(k)</m> holds
                  for every integer <m>k</m> with
                  <m>n_0 \leq k \leq n</m>, then <m>P(n+1)</m> holds.
                </li>
              </ol>
              Then <m>P(n)</m> holds for every integer <m>n \geq n_0</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <proof>
            <p>
              We apply ordinary induction to the statement
              <md>Q(n):\quad P(k) \text{ holds for every integer } k \text{ with } n_0 \leq k \leq n.</md>
            </p>
            <p>
              <alert>Base case.</alert> <m>Q(n_0)</m> says only that
              <m>P(n_0)</m> holds, which is hypothesis (a).
            </p>
            <p>
              <alert>Inductive step.</alert> Assume <m>Q(n)</m> holds for
              some integer <m>n \geq n_0</m>. By hypothesis (b),
              <m>P(n+1)</m> holds. Together with <m>Q(n)</m>, this says that
              <m>P(k)</m> holds for every integer <m>k</m> with
              <m>n_0 \leq k \leq n+1</m>, which is <m>Q(n+1)</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
              By the principle of mathematical induction, <m>Q(n)</m> holds
              for every integer <m>n \geq n_0</m>. Since <m>Q(n)</m>
              includes <m>P(n)</m>, the statement <m>P(n)</m> holds for every
              integer <m>n \geq n_0</m>.
            </p>
          </proof>
        </theorem>
        <p>
          A strong induction proof has the same parts as an ordinary one.
          State the induction hypothesis, which is now <q><m>P(k)</m> holds
          for every <m>k</m> with <m>n_0 \leq k \leq n</m>,</q> and label
          each place you use it, as in <xref ref="ex-mod1-we-4"/>. You will
          use strong induction in <xref ref="ex-mod1-ps-c4"/>.
        </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-sg-z15-4">
        <title>Exhaustive Case Analysis</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Zorn's section <q>Brute Force, Cases, Exhaustion</q> is brief.
            What kind of claim is best handled by exhaustive case analysis?
            Give one example from mathematics (invented or remembered).
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            Case analysis works best when the claim naturally splits into
            a finite number of mutually exclusive, collectively exhaustive
            situations, each of which can be handled individually.
            Example: <q>For any integer <m>n</m>, <m>n^2</m> leaves remainder
            0 or 1 when divided by 4.</q> Proof by cases: either <m>n</m> is
            even (<m>n = 2k</m>, so <m>n^2 = 4k^2 \equiv 0</m>) or <m>n</m>
            is odd (<m>n = 2k+1</m>, so <m>n^2 = 4k^2+4k+1 \equiv 1</m>).
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->

      <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod1-sg-h2">
        <title>Reading 3: Hirst Ch. 2, &#xa7;2.1&#x2013;2.2 and &#xa7;2.4 (Suggested time: 10 minutes)</title>
        <p>
          Hirst formalizes the components of mathematical statements:
          <term>predicates</term> (<m>P(x,y)</m>), <term>terms</term>,
          <term>connectives</term> (<m>\wedge, \vee, \to, \neg</m>), and
          quantifiers. Focus on &#xa7;2.1&#x2013;2.2 (building blocks and
          translations) and &#xa7;2.4 (free and bound variables). Sections
          2.6&#x2013;2.18 are for deeper optional study.
        </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-sg-h2-1">
        <title>Predicates vs. Functions</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            In Hirst's notation, what is the difference between a
            <term>predicate</term> and a <term>function</term>? Why does
            this distinction matter for mathematical statements?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            In the text's setting, a <term>function</term> maps the universe
            to itself (e.g., <m>f(x) = x^2</m> maps reals to reals).
            A <term>predicate</term> maps inputs to truth values&#x2014;it
            is a property that a term either satisfies or does not
            (e.g., <m>P(x) \equiv (x \gt 0)</m>). The distinction matters
            because a function builds an object (a term such as <m>f(x)</m>),
            while a predicate builds a formula (such as <m>P(f(x))</m>).
            A quantifier must be followed by a formula, and it binds its
            variable wherever it occurs in that formula, including inside
            <m>f(x)</m>, as in <m>\forall x\, \bigl(f(x) = 0\bigr)</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-sg-h2-2">
        <title>Quantifier Order with <m>L(x,y)</m></title>
        <statement>
          <p> Hirst gives four statements about <m>L(x,y)</m>
            using mixed quantifiers. Write all four, translate each into
            plain English, and identify which pairs have the same truth
            value when <m>L(x,y)</m> means <q><m>x \lt y</m></q>
            over <m>\R</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            The four statements (with <m>L(x,y): x \lt y</m> over <m>\R</m>):
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                <m>\forall x\, \forall y,\; L(x,y)</m>: <q>Every real is less
                than every real.</q> <em>False.</em>
              </li>
              <li>
                <m>\forall x\, \exists y,\; L(x,y)</m>: <q>Every real has
                some real greater than it.</q> <em>True.</em>
              </li>
              <li>
                <m>\exists x\, \forall y,\; L(x,y)</m>: <q>Some real is less
                than every real.</q> <em>False.</em>
              </li>
              <li>
                <m>\exists x\, \exists y,\; L(x,y)</m>: <q>There exist two
                reals with the first less than the second.</q> <em>True.</em>
              </li>
            </ol>
            Statements (b) and (d) are both true; (a) and (c) are both false.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-sg-h2-3">
        <title>Free and Bound Variables</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            In the formula
            <md>\forall x \bigl(P(x, y) \to \forall y(R(x, y) \vee
            Q(y))\bigr) \wedge B(x),</md>
            identify all free and bound variable occurrences. Is this
            formula a sentence (closed formula)?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <alert>Bound occurrences:</alert> The outermost <m>\forall x</m>
            binds the <m>x</m> in <m>P(x,y)</m> and <m>R(x,y)</m>
            within the first conjunct. The inner <m>\forall y</m> binds
            the <m>y</m> in <m>R(x,y)</m> and <m>Q(y)</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <alert>Free occurrences:</alert> The <m>y</m> in <m>P(x,y)</m>
            is free (it lies outside the scope of <m>\forall y</m>).
            The <m>x</m> in <m>B(x)</m> in the second conjunct
            is free (it lies outside the scope of <m>\forall x</m>).
          </p>
          <p>
            Because both <m>x</m> (in <m>B(x)</m>) and <m>y</m> (in <m>P(x,y)</m>)
            have free occurrences, this formula is <em>not</em> a sentence;
            it is an open formula.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->

      <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod1-sg-h3">
        <title>Reading 4: Hirst Ch. 3, &#xa7;3.5&#x2013;3.6 (Suggested time: 5 minutes)</title>
        <p>
          Use this reading to see how Hirst presents proof strategies
          in a more formal framework. Pay particular attention to
          &#xa7;3.6 (existence, uniqueness, and iff proofs), all of
          which appear regularly in MAT 5610.
        </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-sg-h3-1">
        <title>Other Strategies</title>
        <statement>
          <p> Hirst &#xa7;3.6 lists strategies for proving
            <term>existence</term>, <term>uniqueness</term>,
            <term>iff statements</term>, and <term>equalities</term>.
            Summarize each strategy in one sentence.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                <alert>Existence:</alert> Exhibit a specific object that
                satisfies the required property, or use a non-constructive
                argument (e.g., contradiction) to show one must exist.
              </li>
              <li>
                <alert>Uniqueness:</alert> Assume two objects <m>a</m> and
                <m>b</m> both satisfy the property and show <m>a = b</m>.
              </li>
              <li>
                <alert>Iff (biconditional):</alert> Prove both directions
                separately&#x2014;<m>P \Rightarrow Q</m> and
                <m>Q \Rightarrow P</m>&#x2014;each by any valid method.
              </li>
              <li>
                <alert>Equalities:</alert> Show each side is <m>\leq</m>
                the other (the <q>double inequality</q> method), or
                transform one side into the other by a chain of equalities.
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-sg-h3-2">
        <title>Contradiction vs. Contrapositive</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Hirst gives <q>If <m>n^2</m> is even, then <m>n</m> is even</q>
            as an example of proof by contradiction. But as Hirst notes,
            the same result follows from the contrapositive of a direct
            result. Which approach do you find more natural, and why?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            Both are valid. <alert>Contrapositive:</alert> Assume <m>n</m>
            is odd, so <m>n = 2k+1</m>; then <m>n^2 = 4k^2+4k+1</m> is
            odd. This is clean and direct. <alert>Contradiction:</alert>
            Assume <m>n^2</m> is even but <m>n</m> is odd; the same
            calculation shows <m>n^2</m> is odd, contradiction.
          </p>
          <p>
            Many analysts prefer the contrapositive here because it avoids
            the extra negation overhead; contradiction is more natural when
            there is no obvious contrapositive to prove.
            Either approach is acceptable in MAT 5610.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-sg-h3-3">
        <title>Proving a Conjunction</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            According to Hirst &#xa7;3.6, to prove
            <m>C \to (A \wedge B)</m> directly, what must you show?
            What does the contrapositive look like for this type of statement?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <alert>Direct:</alert> Assuming <m>C</m>, prove both <m>A</m>
            and <m>B</m> separately. Since the conclusion is a conjunction,
            you must establish each conjunct under the hypothesis.
          </p>
          <p>
            <alert>Contrapositive:</alert>
            <m>\neg(A \wedge B) \to \neg C</m>, which by De Morgan is
            <m>(\neg A \vee \neg B) \to \neg C</m>. In practice this is
            proved by cases, and both cases are needed: assume <m>\neg A</m>
            and derive <m>\neg C</m>; then assume <m>\neg B</m> and derive
            <m>\neg C</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->

      <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod1-sg-syn">
        <title>Synthesis: The Convergence Definition</title>
        <p>
          Read the following definition carefully. You will encounter it
          on the first day of MAT 5610.
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Definition (Convergence of a Sequence, Bauldry Ch. 2).</alert>
          A sequence <m>(a_n)_{n=1}^\infty</m> of real numbers
          <term>converges</term> to a limit <m>L \in \R</m>
          if and only if
          <md>
            \forall \varepsilon \gt 0 \;\; \exists N \in \N
            \text{ such that } \forall n \in \N,\;\; n \geq N \implies |a_n - L| \lt \varepsilon.
          </md>
          We write <m>\lim_{n \to \infty} a_n = L</m> or <m>a_n \to L</m>.
        </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-sg-syn-1">
        <title>Parsing the Definition</title>
        <statement>
          <p> Identify the quantifiers, their order, and the
            universe of each variable in the convergence definition.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            The definition contains three quantifiers in this order:
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                <m>\forall \varepsilon \gt 0</m>: universal quantifier over
                positive reals (<m>\varepsilon \in (0,\infty) \subset \R</m>).
              </li>
              <li>
                <m>\exists N \in \N</m>: existential quantifier over the
                natural numbers. Note that <m>N</m> is allowed to depend
                on <m>\varepsilon</m> (it comes after the universal).
              </li>
              <li>
                <m>\forall n \in \N</m>: universal quantifier over the
                natural numbers (the indices), governing the implication
                <m>n \geq N \implies |a_n - L| \lt \varepsilon</m>. Together
                with that implication it says <q>for all <m>n \geq N</m></q>.
              </li>
            </ol>
            The free variables are <m>(a_n)</m> (the sequence) and
            <m>L</m> (the proposed limit): the definition does not quantify
            them, so they are parameters fixed before the statement begins.
            The bound variables are <m>\varepsilon</m>, <m>N</m>, and
            <m>n</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-sg-syn-2">
        <title>Negating the Definition</title>
        <statement>
          <p> Write the negation of the convergence definition
            (i.e., the definition of <m>a_n \not\to L</m>). What does
            each quantifier become?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            Applying the quantifier De Morgan laws:
            <md>
              \exists \varepsilon \gt 0 \;\; \forall N \in \N,\;\;
              \exists n \geq N \text{ such that } |a_n - L| \geq \varepsilon.
            </md>
            This takes two steps. First, each quantifier flips:
            <m>\forall \varepsilon</m> becomes <m>\exists \varepsilon</m>;
            <m>\exists N</m> becomes <m>\forall N</m>; and
            <m>\forall n</m> becomes <m>\exists n</m>. Second, the implication
            <m>n \geq N \Rightarrow |a_n-L| \lt \varepsilon</m> negates, by
            <m>\neg(P \Rightarrow Q) \iff P \wedge \neg Q</m>, to
            <m>n \geq N</m> and <m>|a_n - L| \geq \varepsilon</m>. Combining
            <m>\exists n \in \N</m> with <m>n \geq N</m> gives
            <m>\exists n \geq N</m> with <m>|a_n - L| \geq \varepsilon</m>.
            In words: the sequence does not converge to <m>L</m> if there
            is some <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m> such that no matter how far out
            you go, you can find a term at least <m>\varepsilon</m> away from
            <m>L</m>.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-sg-syn-3">
        <title>Formulating Divergence</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The definition says a sequence <em>converges to a specific
            limit</em> <m>L</m>. Suppose you want to state that a
            sequence diverges (does not converge to <em>any</em> limit).
            How would you formulate this using your quantifier rules?
            (Hint: which symbol does <m>L</m> become?)
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            A sequence <m>(a_n)</m> <term>diverges</term> if it converges
            to no limit, i.e.:
            <md>
              \forall L \in \R,\;\;
              \exists \varepsilon \gt 0 \;\; \forall N \in \N,\;\;
              \exists n \geq N \text{ such that } |a_n - L| \geq \varepsilon.
            </md>
            The formerly-free variable <m>L</m> becomes universally quantified
            at the outermost level: for <em>every</em> candidate limit, the
            sequence fails to converge to it.
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>


  </section>
